# Quantum proton tunneling and QPAM sonification

This repository simulates hydrogen tunneling in the active site of human carbonic anhydrase II (PDB 1HED) and sonifies the statevector with Moth Quantum's quantumaudio package.

## Units and grid

- Keep every internal calculation in SI units: metres, kilograms, seconds, joules.
- Convert to angstroms, electron-volts, and femtoseconds only at the plot and slider boundary.
- Use the constants in `src/hamiltonian.py` (ħ, proton mass, joule per electron-volt). Do not switch the solver to ħ = 1.
- The reaction coordinate is an 8-qubit register. 2^8 = 256 spatial samples on x ∈ [-1.5 Å, +1.5 Å].
- Build the Hamiltonian as a sparse matrix H = T + V. Form the dense unitary only when applying U(dt) = exp(−i H dt / ħ).

## Moth Quantum audio

- Encode audio with Quantum Probability Amplitude Modulation (QPAM).
- Call `quantumaudio.encode(samples, scheme="qpam")` and `quantumaudio.decode(circuit)`.
- Samples passed to QPAM must be real and inside [-1, 1]. Project the complex statevector onto a real quadrature before encoding; QPAM's converter casts to real probability amplitudes.
- Report circuit depth, statevector fidelity, and the backend that actually ran. Do not invent a successful Atlas HTTP status.
- Dispatch the job payload to the Atlas API when `ATLAS_API_URL` / `ATLAS_API_KEY` are set, and always keep the local AerSimulator result.

## Application

- The entry point is `streamlit run app.py`.
- Sliders: barrier 0.1–1.0 eV, asymmetry 0.0–0.2 eV, duration 5–50 fs, plus a base audio pitch.
- The audio control label is `🎵 Synthesize Quantum Audio (QPAM)`.
- The platform card title is `🌐 Moth Atlas Platform API Output`.
